{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42472 - PROTEIN:4911",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which N-Methyltyramine may interact with CD276 antigen through a series of enzymatic and metabolic pathways. N-Methyltyramine is identified as a target of multiple compounds, including 6-Hydroxy-2-[2-(2-hydroxyphenyl) ethyl]chromone, Erysimoside, and Flavipin, all of which are known to target the Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9. This family of enzymes is involved in various metabolic processes, and their inhibition may modulate the expression or activity of CD276 antigen. The presence of multiple independent pathways linking N-Methyltyramine to CD276 antigen underscores a potential role in the regulation of this antigen through the action of these compounds. The evidence supports a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of SDR family members in mediating the effects of N-Methyltyramine on CD276 antigen, suggesting a possible role in cellular signaling or immune modulation.",
